WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:05.520
Speaker 1: Today, I'm going to talk about
the hypocrisy of Republicans when it comes

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:12.030
to business freedom that they claim to espouse
and low regulations which they claim to support.

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:16.470
And this is all kind of coming on the heels
of yesterday's Ron DeSantis Disneyworld.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:20.270
I'll build a prison next to Disney.

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:26.240
Threat and controversy, which is really just
one of dozens and dozens of data points of

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:31.970
Republicans who claim to be about business
freedom and low regulation, immediately abandoning

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:37.170
that principle to the extent that it's even
a principle as soon as it's politically inconvenient.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:43.539
Now, in order to understand where this hypocrisy
comes from and why it's so glaring today,

00:00:43.539 --> 00:00:48.260
you really have to understand the history
of the Republican Party As for decades and

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:53.199
decades and decades, claiming to be the party
of business, freedom of low regulations, of

00:00:53.199 --> 00:01:00.000
economic freedom and pro-business and all
of these things they said for decades.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.570
Excessive regulation is a burden on businesses
and it hurts profits and it stifles economic

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:07.990
growth and therefore it prevents hiring.

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:14.030
And the best thing we can do for workers and
for the economy and for investors for everybody,

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:19.759
is to keep regulations low and stay out of
the way of businesses.

00:01:19.759 --> 00:01:25.330
Let the market decide which businesses succeed
and which businesses fail.

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:30.200
The truth is so much more pathetic.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:37.040
It's we support business freedom in theory,
when it suits our interests and doesn't conflict

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:42.540
with personal vendettas, grievances, or other
inconvenient realities.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:47.580
Now, let's take a look at some examples and
again, understand that there was a period,

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:55.880
even if you disagree with this obsessive focus
on unburdening businesses of regulations,

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:57.420
you might disagree with that.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:03.920
We can still acknowledge that at a certain
point, conservatives, as they once were, were

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:05.810
actually much more committed to that.

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:09.460
I would still disagree with them in many cases
and say, no, we need regulation.

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:11.910
But there was a point at which when they said
it, they meant it.

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:12.910
That's not the case anymore.

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:13.910
Twitter.

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:15.380
Twitter is a recent example.

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:19.480
That's a glaring one when it comes to Twitter.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:25.230
Many of these right wingers immediately abandoned
their stated principles of getting rid of

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:31.200
burdensome regulations and separating government
from what private companies do, and said no,

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:35.900
Twitter should be forced to publish my COVID
disinformation.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:41.440
Twitter should be forced to publish Hunter
Biden nude pics which violate their own terms

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:42.459
of service.

00:02:42.459 --> 00:02:49.590
Twitter should be forced to let governments
decide or the government decide what hateful,

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:53.910
idle ideology or hateful speech should be
published.

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:59.000
Rather than saying Let Twitter figure out
what it wants to do, let Twitter figure out

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:00.569
what's good or bad for business.

00:03:00.569 --> 00:03:07.000
Let Twitter make its decisions, assuming they're
not illegal and let the market of ideas and

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:11.650
the marketplace of people decide, Oh, I don't
like what Twitter is doing, so I'm not going

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:13.730
to be on Twitter anymore.

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:16.629
That's the way that they claim they operate.

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:21.320
But as Twitter showed us, that's not actually
the way they operate when they want to force

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:24.739
their disinformation onto a private platform.

00:03:24.739 --> 00:03:31.030
Twitter Republicans at one point pushed a
law that would have prevented states from

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:34.760
enacting their own regulations on certain
products.

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:40.569
They would argue, oh, you know, we need to
protect businesses from a patchwork of different

00:03:40.569 --> 00:03:42.709
regulations from state to state.

00:03:42.709 --> 00:03:47.709
It's burdensome to business to say, well,
we're going to regulate your product this

00:03:47.709 --> 00:03:51.650
way in Delaware, but in New York, it's going
to be different.

00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:53.590
And then in Florida, it's going to be different.

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:56.659
So they pushed a law that said, you can't
do that.

00:03:56.659 --> 00:04:00.840
You can't just let states regulate products
differently in each state.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:06.230
But when it comes to environmental regulations,
Republicans don't care about a patchwork of

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:07.230
regulations.

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:13.069
They want to strip the EPA's federal power,
for example, to regulate greenhouse gas emissions

00:04:13.069 --> 00:04:14.310
at the federal level.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:15.310
And they listen.

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:17.290
There are states that aren't worried about
that.

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:22.019
We know if a state isn't concerned, if you
have a state like Montana, for example, Jared

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:24.460
Diamond has written about this a lot.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:32.110
If Montana wants to let the, you know, the
soil and the land and every water be ravaged

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:35.640
by pollution or Wyoming, let them do it.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:41.700
You can't have this oppressive EPA come in
and tell every state what they have to do.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:45.320
Well, that's the principle that you are now
going against.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:47.550
You're against the patchwork of laws.

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:50.590
But now you're okay with the patchwork of
laws.

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:54.881
If it might prevent the regulation of greenhouse
gases, they're willing to do it 50 different

00:04:54.881 --> 00:04:59.949
ways and each of the 50 states, because they
want to make it difficult to regulate greenhouse

00:04:59.949 --> 00:05:01.169
gas emissions.

00:05:01.169 --> 00:05:02.169
Then we've got subsidies.

00:05:02.169 --> 00:05:08.139
And this is, you know, each one of these things
I mentioning is a category unto itself with

00:05:08.139 --> 00:05:09.880
many examples we could go into.

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:12.430
But let's now talk about subsidies.

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:18.400
The right claims to be against government
intervention in the economy, picking winners

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:20.759
and losers, etc.

00:05:20.759 --> 00:05:26.230
But they have no problem handing out billions
of dollars in subsidies to certain industries,

00:05:26.230 --> 00:05:31.690
including oil and others, when they are politically
convenient businesses.

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:38.250
When it comes to labor regulations, Republicans
suddenly are big fans of government intervention.

00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:46.889
They love the idea of how can we interfere
with business to prevent workers from organizing

00:05:46.889 --> 00:05:49.300
and forming unions, for example.

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:55.400
They support laws that make it more difficult
for workers to sue employers when there are

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:56.810
workplace violations.

00:05:56.810 --> 00:06:01.180
You know, if you don't want to regulate the
businesses because you claim, oh, they have

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:06.570
a they have an incentive to regulate themselves,
and if they put out a dangerous product, they'll

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:09.070
get sued and they'll lose market share.

00:06:09.070 --> 00:06:12.990
And if they don't have good working conditions
for their employees, their employees will

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:14.350
sue or quit.

00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:19.930
Well, but then they also want to make it more
difficult for the employees to sue because

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:21.860
it's in their interests.

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:28.460
The truth, as many of you have long suspected,
is that Republicans only care about business

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:35.919
freedom and unburdening from burdensome regulations
when it serves their interests.

00:06:35.919 --> 00:06:40.690
When it comes to protecting the environment
or protecting workers.

00:06:40.690 --> 00:06:45.110
Suddenly, regulations are a burden that need
to be lifted.

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:49.509
This or you need to put in a regulation.

00:06:49.509 --> 00:06:55.740
You need to put in a regulation that prevents
others from putting in regulations.

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:58.320
It's whatever is convenient at the time.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:01.990
And this, at the end of the day, is bad for
the economy.

00:07:01.990 --> 00:07:06.610
It's bad for the sort of average health of
people as they are affected by the lack of

00:07:06.610 --> 00:07:11.889
regulation or a very oppressive type of regulation,
depending on who it is that benefits and who

00:07:11.889 --> 00:07:14.199
is hurt, the wellbeing of workers, the environment.

00:07:14.199 --> 00:07:15.669
I mean, you all know it.

00:07:15.669 --> 00:07:22.139
So we're going to be going through over the
next several weeks, each of these stated principles

00:07:22.139 --> 00:07:28.250
unburden ourselves from regulation, except
when you want to force your will onto corporations.

00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:29.720
We're going to look at each of these.

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:36.199
And there are so many of these stated principles
that they claim but don't actually follow.

00:07:36.199 --> 00:07:37.710
This is the first one.

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:39.500
Business freedom, low regulation.

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:41.380
We'll get to others over the next few weeks.

